VK, D3D12, validation, shared: an object allocation that fails answers OUT_OF_MEMORY.

Allocate returns null when AllocationCallbacks::Allocate does, but most of its callers use the
result unchecked: CreateImplementation calls Create on it (since 2104586), and so do device
creation, the command allocators, swap chains, Imgui, Streamer and Upscaler (with its NIS, FFX,
XeSS, NGX and MetalFX parts); the validation layer returns SUCCESS with a null object when its
wrapper allocation fails, leaking the backend object. Newer code checks: the WebGPU backend's
CreateImplementation, the transfer contexts, descriptor heaps and video objects.

This checks every Allocate in those paths, as the WebGPU backend does: a failed allocation answers
OUT_OF_MEMORY, destroys what the call created before it, and leaves the out-pointer null. On D3D12 a
swap chain texture whose Create fails is destroyed too (it leaked). NRI's containers (Vector and the
rest, through StdAllocator, which is noexcept) still cannot report a null; that is unchanged, so an
allocator may still not fail inside one, and device creation's first allocation is one
(nriCreateDevice's Vector<QueueFamilyDesc>).
Measured on v190, NVIDIA: an allocator returning null for CreateFence's allocation ended the process
with an access violation (0xC0000005), on Vulkan and D3D12, with NRI validation on and off. With the
change, failing each allocation of CreateFence, CreateBuffer, CreateTexture, AllocateMemory,
CreateCommandAllocator, CreateCommandBuffer, CreateQueryPool and CreateSampler in turn (two each with
NRI validation, one without) answers OUT_OF_MEMORY every time, leaks nothing, and leaves the device
working, on both backends.

